w. Set the junctions so every parcel lands in its own bin.","scene.routing.b.reveal":"You chose which colour each junction pulls aside, and the rest followed.","scene.routing.c.objective":"Some junctions flip after every parcel. Set them all so each parcel lands in its own bin.","scene.routing.c.reveal":"You planned for junctions that change as they go, and it still came out sorted.","scene.locks.a.objective":"Turn the dials until each one points at its mark, then open the lock.","scene.locks.a.reveal":"You found the turns that brought both dials to their marks.","scene.locks.b.objective":"Three linked dials. Bring each one to its mark, then open the lock.","scene.locks.b.reveal":"You worked out which dial drags which, and turned them in an order that works.","scene.locks.c.objective":"Every dial pulls on others. Bring them all to their marks, then open the lock.","scene.locks.c.reveal":"You combined turns that undo each other's side effects until every dial sat on its mark.","scene.gears.a.objective":"Fit a gear so the hand goes round exactly {turns}× while the crank goes round {crank}×.","scene.gears.a.reveal":"You picked the gear whose size makes the hand keep pace exactly.","scene.gears.b.objective":"Fit the gears so the hand goes round exactly {turns}× while the crank goes round {crank}×.","scene.gears.b.reveal":"You chose gears whose sizes together give exactly the right speed.","scene.gears.c.objective":"Fit every gear so the hand goes round exactly {turns}× while the crank goes round {crank}×.","scene.gears.c.reveal":"You built a chain of gears that, step by step, reaches the exact speed.","scene.cargo.a.objective":"Load every crate into the hold.","scene.cargo.a.reveal":"You turned and placed the crates until they filled the hold with no gaps.","scene.cargo.b.objective":"Load every crate and keep the ship level. Dotted crates are heavy.","scene.cargo.b.reveal":"You filled the hold and kept the heavy crates balanced across the middle.","scene.cargo.c.objective":"Part of the hold is blocked. Load every crate and keep the ship level.","scene.cargo.c.reveal":"You fitted everything around the blocked space and still kept the ship level.","hint.balance.a.1":"A crate pulls harder the farther out it hangs.","hint.balance.a.2":"A light crate far out can match a heavy crate close in.","hint.balance.a.3":"Count the hooks from the middle on each side. Heavy and near can equal light and far.","hint.balance.b.1":"Every crate adds its own pull. The two sides only need the same total.","hint.balance.b.2":"Try the heaviest crate first, close to the middle. Then adjust with the light ones.","hint.balance.b.3":"For each crate, think of its weight and its hooks from the middle together. Make the two sides add up equal.","hint.balance.c.1":"Broken hooks can't be used. Look at what is left on each side first.","hint.balance.c.2":"Work out how much pull one side still needs. Then see which crate and hook give exactly that.","hint.balance.c.3":"If nothing fits, swap two crates. A small move far out changes more than a big move near the middle.","hint.ray.a.1":"The beam leaves a mirror at the same slant it arrived.","hint.ray.a.2":"Turn the mirror a little and watch which way the beam swings.","hint.ray.a.3":"Face the mirror halfway between where the beam comes from and where it must go.","hint.ray.b.1":"Sort out the first mirror the beam meets before touching the next.","hint.ray.b.2":"Each mirror only has to send the beam on to the next one.","hint.ray.b.3":"Work backwards. Which mirror can see the sensor? Aim the beam at that mirror first.","hint.ray.c.1":"Walls stop the beam. Mirrors are the only way around them.","hint.ray.c.2":"Find a clear line into the sensor, then get the beam onto that line.","hint.ray.c.3":"Check if the beam can bounce twice before it reaches the sensor. The long way round may be the only open one.","hint.shadow.a.1":"Move the lamp one way and the shadow slides the other way.","hint.shadow.a.2":"The shadow always falls on the far side of the board from the lamp.","hint.shadow.a.3":"Imagine a straight string from the lamp past each edge of the board. Where the strings touch the ground is where the shadow ends.","hint.shadow.b.1":"A low lamp throws a long shadow. A high lamp throws a short one.","hint.shadow.b.2":"Get the shadow the right width first by raising or lowering the lamp. Then slide it into place.","hint.shadow.b.3":"When the lamp hangs twice as high as the board, the shadow is twice as wide as the board.","hint.shadow.c.1":"The lamp's height sets how much bigger the shadow is than the board.","hint.shadow.c.2":"Fix the size with the lamp's height. Then use the board and the lamp's position to place the shadow.","hint.shadow.c.3":"Sliding the board moves the shadow the same way. Sliding the lamp moves it the opposite way.","hint.flow.a.1":"A wider-open tap fills its tank faster.","hint.flow.a.2":"The timer runs the same for every tank. Only the taps differ.","hint.flow.a.3":"Run it once and see how far the water gets. Opening the tap twice as far gives twice the height.","hint.flow.b.1":"A wide tank needs more water to rise the same height.","hint.flow.b.2":"A tank twice as wide rises half as fast from the same tap.","hint.flow.b.3":"For each tank, look at its width and its line. A wide tank with a high line needs the most open tap.","hint.flow.c.1":"A longer timer raises every tank. The taps set how the tanks differ from each other.","hint.flow.c.2":"First use the taps to get the tanks right compared with each other. Then use the timer to lift them all.","hint.flow.c.3":"If the pump is weak, opening everything wide makes every tap weaker. Open only what you need.","hint.routing.a.1":"Follow one parcel with your finger from the top to see where it ends up.","hint.routing.a.2":"A plain junction sends everything the same way. A colour junction pulls one colour aside.","hint.routing.a.3":"Start at the bins. For each bin, which way must the junction above it send that colour?","hint.routing.b.1":"A colour junction sends its own colour left and all the others right.","hint.routing.b.2":"Decide which colour must leave the line first, and set the top junction to it.","hint.routing.b.3":"Once one colour is pulled aside, the junctions below only have to deal with what is left.","hint.routing.c.1":"A flipping junction sends one parcel left, the next one right, then left again.","hint.routing.c.2":"Look at the order the parcels arrive in. A flip only matters for the parcels that reach it.","hint.routing.c.3":"Count which parcels reach a flipping junction, in order. Set its first side so that the first of them goes the right way.","hint.locks.a.1":"Turning one dial drags the other along. Watch which one follows.","hint.locks.a.2":"Set the dial that moves others first. Finish with the one that moves alone.","hint.locks.a.3":"A dial that goes all the way round ends where it began. One click back is the same as going forward almost a full circle.","hint.locks.b.1":"Turn each dial once and notice which others move with it.","hint.locks.b.2":"Find a dial that moves only itself. Leave that one for last.","hint.locks.b.3":"Fix the dials in order. Start with the one nothing else can correct later, and end with the one that turns alone.","hint.locks.c.1":"Some dials turn others backwards. Check the direction, not only which ones move.","hint.locks.c.2":"The order of your turns does not change where the dials end up. Only how many times you turn each one matters.","hint.locks.c.3":"Pick one dial and bring it to its mark. Then use dials that don't disturb it to fix the rest.","hint.gears.a.1":"A small gear pushed by a big one spins faster.","hint.gears.a.2":"Count the teeth. A gear with half the teeth turns twice as often.","hint.gears.a.3":"Compare the teeth of the two gears that touch. If one has three times the teeth, the other turns three times as often.","hint.gears.b.1":"It is the pair that matters: the gear that pushes and the gear being pushed.","hint.gears.b.2":"To speed the hand up, let a big gear push a small one. To slow it down, do the opposite.","hint.gears.b.3":"Two pairs in a row build on each other. Doubling the speed twice gives four times the speed.","hint.gears.c.1":"First judge roughly how much faster or slower the hand must go than the crank.","hint.gears.c.2":"Split the job between the pairs. One pair can do most of it and the other the rest.","hint.gears.c.3":"For each pair, compare the pusher's teeth with the pushed gear's teeth. All the pairs together must give the hand's turns compared with the crank's turns.","hint.cargo.a.1":"Tap a crate to turn it. Long crates often fit better the other way.","hint.cargo.a.2":"Place the biggest crates first. The small ones fill what is left.","hint.cargo.a.3":"Count the squares. The crates cover the hold exactly, so any gap means something must move.","hint.cargo.b.1":"Heavy crates near one side make the ship lean that way.","hint.cargo.b.2":"Put heavy crates near the middle, or one on each side at the same distance.","hint.cargo.b.3":"How hard a crate tips the ship depends on its weight and how far it sits from the middle. Make the left and right add up the same.","hint.cargo.c.1":"Blocked squares can't hold crates. Fit the awkward corners first.","hint.cargo.c.2":"Get every crate aboard first. Then swap crates of the same shape to fix the lean.","hint.cargo.c.3":"Swapping a heavy crate with a light one of the same size keeps the fit and changes the balance.","concept.balance_a.name":"The law of the lever","concept.balance_a.idea":"Weight times distance from the pivot was the same on both sides. That product is called a moment.","concept.balance_a.seen":"Seesaws, wheelbarrows, door handles","concept.balance_b.name":"Adding moments","concept.balance_b.idea":"Each crate adds its weight times its distance to its side. The arm is level when the two sums match.","concept.balance_b.seen":"Loading an aeroplane, a hanging mobile, kitchen scales","concept.balance_c.name":"Solving for the unknown","concept.balance_c.idea":"You had a total to reach and limited choices, and found the values that make both sides equal. That is solving an equation.","concept.balance_c.seen":"Balancing a budget, adjusting a recipe, trimming a boat","concept.ray_a.name":"The law of reflection","concept.ray_a.idea":"Light bounces off a flat mirror at the same angle it hits it.","concept.ray_a.seen":"Rear-view mirrors, periscopes, a ball off a cushion","concept.ray_b.name":"Chained reflections","concept.ray_b.idea":"Every bounce follows the same rule, so a whole path can be built one angle at a time.","concept.ray_b.seen":"Kaleidoscopes, laser shows, trick shots in pool","concept.ray_c.name":"Paths made of angles","concept.ray_c.idea":"By choosing the angle at each mirror you planned a route around obstacles. Geometry did the steering.","concept.ray_c.seen":"Fibre-optic cables, sound in a concert hall, radar","concept.shadow_a.name":"Projection","concept.shadow_a.idea":"Light travels in straight lines, so a shadow is the board's outline carried along those lines to the ground.","concept.shadow_a.seen":"Sundials, shadow puppets, film projectors","concept.shadow_b.name":"Similar triangles","concept.shadow_b.idea":"The lamp, the board and its shadow make two triangles of the same shape. Their sizes stay in step, so the lamp's height sets the shadow's width.","concept.shadow_b.seen":"Measuring a tree by its shadow, zooming a projector, long evening shadows","concept.shadow_c.name":"Scale and proportion","concept.shadow_c.idea":"The shadow is the board enlarged by a fixed ratio set by the two heights. Change the ratio and every length changes together.","concept.shadow_c.seen":"Maps and plans, enlarging a photo, a torch held near a wall","concept.flow_a.name":"Rate and time","concept.flow_a.idea":"How much you get is how fast it flows multiplied by how long it runs.","concept.flow_a.seen":"Filling a bucket, charging a phone, driving at a steady speed","concept.flow_b.name":"Volume and height","concept.flow_b.idea":"The water's volume is the tank's base times its height, so the same volume stands lower in a wider tank.","concept.flow_b.seen":"Pouring a drink into a wide glass, filling a bath, rain gauges","concept.flow_c.name":"Sharing a fixed supply","concept.flow_c.idea":"There was only so much flow to go round. You split it in the right proportions and chose the time to match.","concept.flow_c.seen":"Water pressure when two taps run, sharing a budget, splitting an internet connection","concept.routing_a.name":"Decision trees","concept.routing_a.idea":"Each junction is a yes-or-no question. A chain of them can sort anything into the right place.","concept.routing_a.seen":"Sorting the post, a quiz flowchart, the game of twenty questions","concept.routing_b.name":"Sorting by rules","concept.routing_b.idea":"If it is this colour, go left; otherwise carry on. Simple rules in the right order sort a whole mixture.","concept.routing_b.seen":"Recycling plants, email filters, coin sorters","concept.routing_c.name":"Machines with memory","concept.routing_c.idea":"A flipping junction remembers what it did last. Systems whose next move depends on their past are called state machines.","concept.routing_c.seen":"Traffic lights, turnstiles, a light switch","concept.locks_a.name":"Clock arithmetic","concept.locks_a.idea":"On a dial, counting wraps around: past the last position you are back at the first. This is called modular arithmetic.","concept.locks_a.seen":"Clock faces, days of the week, combination locks","concept.locks_b.name":"Linked equations","concept.locks_b.idea":"Each turn changed several dials at once. Finding how many turns of each one gives the right totals is solving several equations together.","concept.locks_b.seen":"Mixing paint colours, a twisting cube puzzle, adjusting a recipe","concept.locks_c.name":"Combining moves","concept.locks_c.idea":"Turns add up the same in any order, so you could treat each dial's turns as a number and find the mix that lands every dial at once.","concept.locks_c.seen":"Puzzle boxes, sliders on a sound mixer, tuning a guitar","concept.gears_a.name":"Ratios","concept.gears_a.idea":"Two meshed gears turn in the ratio of their teeth, flipped over: fewer teeth means more turns.","concept.gears_a.seen":"Bicycle gears, hand whisks, clockwork toys","concept.gears_b.name":"Multiplying ratios","concept.gears_b.idea":"Each pair of gears multiplies the speed by its own ratio, and pairs in a row multiply together.","concept.gears_b.seen":"Car gearboxes, wind-up clocks, drill speeds","concept.gears_c.name":"Gear trains","concept.gears_c.idea":"A long chain of gears is several ratios multiplied together. Choosing them means breaking the speed you want into factors.","concept.gears_c.seen":"Mechanical watches, wind turbines, a tower clock","concept.cargo_a.name":"Area and tiling","concept.cargo_a.idea":"The crates' areas add up to the hold's area. Covering a space exactly with shapes is called tiling.","concept.cargo_a.seen":"Packing a suitcase, laying floor tiles, loading a van","concept.cargo_b.name":"Centre of mass","concept.cargo_b.idea":"The ship sits level when the load's balance point is over the middle. That point is its centre of mass.","concept.cargo_b.seen":"Carrying two shopping bags, loading a lorry, a tightrope walker's pole","concept.cargo_c.name":"Packing with limits","concept.cargo_c.idea":"You had to meet two rules at once: everything fits, and the weight balances. Problems like this are solved by narrowing the choices step by step.","concept.cargo_c.seen":"Container ships, loading aircraft, arranging furniture"}
